# Sprint 35, Req 1a: this file is now intentionally inert, kept only so
# the reasoning is discoverable in the one place someone reasoning about
# packaging would look. package.json's own "files" array is the sole
# packaging mechanism as of this sprint — established by inspecting real
# packed tarballs, not by reasoning about precedence:
#
#   - A `files` array is a complete allowlist on its own. A path NOT
#     listed there does not ship, whether or not this file exists,
#     whether or not it says anything about that path, and regardless of
#     .gitignore (which `files`, unlike this file, never falls back to).
#     Confirmed directly: docs/sprints/ content that was never in `files`
#     did not ship in a real `npm pack`, with no .npmignore present at
#     all.
#   - This file does NOT filter inside a directory `files` already lists
#     wholesale. Confirmed directly: a file excluded here, sitting inside
#     a directory package.json's `files` array includes as a whole entry
#     (e.g. scripts/launcher), still shipped in a real, non-dry-run
#     tarball. This is exactly the "silently fight" scenario Req 1a
#     named as the risk: had docs/sprints stayed a single directory entry
#     in `files` with this file still relied on to exclude real sprint
#     content inside it, that content would ship, silently, the moment
#     any of it existed at publish time — the old fail-open defect,
#     recreated one layer up. Fixed instead by never listing docs/sprints
#     as a directory in `files` at all: it lists the seven phase-folder
#     .gitkeep placeholders install.js actually reads, individually, and
#     nothing else — SPRINT_SKELETON_FILES in scripts/install.js is the
#     same list, which is what makes this repo's own real sprint content
#     structurally unable to ship regardless of what this file says.
#
# Kept rather than deleted so a future "why is there no .npmignore"
# question finds this instead of silence. The __pycache__/*.pyc/.DS_Store
# patterns this file used to carry needed no replacement either:
# __pycache__ and *.pyc were never listed in `files` and so were already
# unreachable by the same allowlist logic, and .DS_Store is excluded by
# npm's own built-in defaults independent of any ignore file — confirmed
# by packing a real tarball with no .npmignore present and a planted
# .DS_Store inside an included directory; it did not ship.
